# libubootenv (fw_printenv / fw_setenv) config for the Beep's U-Boot environment.
#
# ✅ GEOMETRY PROVEN ON HARDWARE (2026-09-09, unit beep-023545 over SSH). The env is
# REDUNDANT: two copies in mtd1 — env0 @ 0x0000, env1 @ 0x8000, each 0x8000, with a
# 0x8000 erase block (the w25q128 does sub-64K erase). Each copy is [CRC32(4) + flags(1)
# + data]. Only the two-line redundant form below reads a VALID CRC:
#     beep_primary  = 0x9f050000   ✓
#     beep_recovery = 0x9f050000   ✓
# The earlier single-line / size-0x8000 / sector-0x10000 guess gave "Bad CRC, using
# default environment" (libubootenv parsed it as non-redundant and CRC'd the wrong span).
#
# ⚠️⚠️ DO NOT run fw_setenv against this config expecting to reset the bootcount, and do
# NOT re-arm the fw_setenv-based good-boot reset. The bootcount counter OVERLAPS env1:
# the wear-leveled 3-strikes byte lives at flash 0x8000 (proven: 0xFF-region with a
# single 0x7f = strike 1), which is exactly libubootenv's env1 slot. A redundant
# fw_setenv writes the *obsolete* copy = env1 @ 0x8000, which erases + overwrites the
# bootcount region with env bytes — it does NOT cleanly reset the count, it corrupts it.
# fw_setenv here is READ-ONLY-safe; treat writes as hazardous. Resetting the bootcount
# must instead replicate the vendor `beepupdate` method: a direct single-nibble bit-clear
# at 0x8000 on /dev/mtd1 (no fw_setenv, no full erase). See docs/RECOVERY-DESIGN.md and
# notes/bootcount-reset-v2-spec.md.
#
# NOTE: /dev/mtd1 assumes the stock mtd order (mtd0 u-boot, mtd1 u-boot-env). If a
# variant reorders partitions this must be re-pointed by name from /proc/mtd.
#
# MTD device   Device offset   Env. size   Flash sector size
/dev/mtd1      0x0000          0x8000      0x8000
/dev/mtd1      0x8000          0x8000      0x8000
